The test methods all have a general setup which involves setting up assumptions and observations for later use. These assumptions can sometimes be static but are usually based on the specific contracts that your code might adhere to. Observations are used to understand how the methods are interacting with each other and can also provide insight in how the classes are treating the inputs/outputs.

**Please note the following points:**

- This framework is used particularly for C# and its main advantage is in assisting in static code analysis and refactoring with a highly intuitive graphical interface.
  
- Pex is based on .NET 4.0 and above; it doesn't work with .NET 2.0, 3.0 or 3.5.
  
- If you are interested in using Pex, ensure your assembly is not marked as ‘InternalsVisibleTo’ a friend assembly. It could cause it to fail at runtime due to security restriction of internal types and members.
  
- Pex isn’t suitable to replace all testing. It is designed to assist in testing, not all possible scenarios. It can test methods for which you have “concepts” or some testing is automated, but if you still have a lot left to test, then unit tests or other types of testing are more effective.
  
- It can be run against code that uses Reflection, in certain complex scenarios.
  
